    "https://careers.uwf.edu/jobs/assistant-professor-104380-pensacola-main-campus-florida-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor 104380",
      "description": "Assistant Professor 104380 - Pensacola - Main Campus, Florida, United States My settings Life at UWF About UWF UWF News Pensacola Area Benefits Wellness Program Search Jobs Job Alerts Faculty Jobs Staff Jobs Adjunct Jobs Temporary Jobs Student Jobs Applicant FAQs My Application Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Please review job requirements carefully. Failure to submit all pieces of the application may result in disqualification. Applications are accepted on the University of West Florida career site . For assistance contact UWF Human Resources at 850.474.2694 or jobs@uwf.edu . Assistant Professor 104380 Pensacola - Main Campus, Florida, United States Teaching, Leadership and Research School of Education Tenured and Tenure-Track Faculty Faculty Full-time Add to favorites View favorites POSITION DETAILS Employment based visa sponsorship will be considered for this position. Position Number: 104380 Classification Title: Assistant Professor of Professional/Clinical Practice Working Title: Assistant Professor of Social Studies Department: Teaching, Leadership and Research FLSA Status: Exempt Salary Range: Commensurate with education and experience Pay Basis: Annually POSITION OVERVIEW JOB SUMMARY: The School of Education at the University of West Florida (UWF) invites applications for a tenure-track Assistant Professor of Social Studies Education to support undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral education programs. The successful candidate will contribute to teaching, research, and service with knowledge of elementary (K–6) social studies teaching and learning, civic education, inquiry-based and interdisciplinary instruction, and the preparation of preservice and in-service teachers. This position focuses on strengthening social studies preparation within the elementary education program, supporting graduate and doctoral student mentoring, and advancing the School’s mission of preparing effective teachers through strong partnerships with K–12 schools and communities. The ideal candidate will demonstrate the ability to connect research and practice in elementary social studies, integrating civics, history, geography, and economics with literacy and other content areas, in ways that prepare teachers to serve all learners in Northwest Florida and beyond. The salary for this position will be commensurate with experience. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: - Earned doctorate (Ph.D. or Ed.D.) in Social Studies Education, Curriculum and Instruction, Teacher Education, or a closely related field by start date. - At least three years of K–12 classroom teaching experience, which includes social studies. - Evidence of effective teaching or strong teaching potential at the postsecondary level, including preparing preservice elementary teachers. - Demonstrated knowledge of elementary social studies content, standards, and pedagogy. - Demonstrated or emerging scholarly agenda in social studies education appropriate for a tenure-track appointment. - Demonstrated ability to work effectively with students, teachers, schools, and communities representing a wide range of backgrounds, experiences, and academic needs. POSITION QUALIFICATIONS: Primary Teaching & Clinical Responsibilities: 1. Teaching & Curriculum Development a. Teach undergraduate courses in elementary social studies methods, curriculum, and instruction, and related teacher education areas in face-to-face and online formats b. Contribute to curriculum revision, alignment, and continuous improvement across elementary education and teacher preparation programs, including alignment with the Florida Educator Accomplished Practices and state K–12 social studies standards c. Model inquiry-based, interdisciplinary approaches that integrate civics, history, geography, and economics with literacy and other content areas d. Support clinical learning experiences, including practicum and internship supervision in partner elementary schools 2. Research & Scholarly Activity a. Develop a scholarly agenda centered on elementary social studies education, such as civic education, history, or geography education, teacher preparation, curriculum development, or interdisciplinary teaching and learning b. Engage in applied and practice-based research, including collaborative projects with PK–12 schools, school districts, museums, and community partners c. Seek external funding (e.g., IES, NSF, NEH, state, or foundation grants), commensurate with rank d. Disseminate scholarship through peer-reviewed publications, presentations, and practitioner outlets 3. Student Mentoring & Advising a. Serve on and progressively chair doctoral dissertation committees, consistent with tenure and departmental expectations b. Support student professional development and career pathways into the teaching profession 4. Service & Engagement a. Participate in department, school, and university service activities b. Contribute to program coordination, assessment, state program approval, and accreditation c. Develop and maintain partnerships with K–12 schools, school districts, and professional organizations (e.g., NCSS, Florida Council for the Social Studies) d. Collaborate with faculty across the School of Education and the university, including colleagues in history, political science, and humanities. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: - At least three years of elementary (K–6) classroom experience, which includes social studies. - Evidence of leadership in elementary social studies education through scholarship, curriculum development, school partnerships, or professional organizations - Expertise in civic education; history, geography, or economics education; inquiry-based instruction (e.g., the C3 Framework); or the integration of social studies with literacy and other content areas. - Experience supervising clinical field experiences and familiarity with Florida educator preparation, teacher certification, and K–12 social studies standards - Graduate and doctoral student advisement and supervision. - Interest in applied research, external funding, and collaborative school and community partnerships. - Ability to teach effectively in face-to-face and online formats. PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Physical Requirements: Occasional and/or light lifting required. Limited exposure to elements such as heat, cold, noise, dust, dirt, chemicals, etc., but none to the point of being disagreeable. May involve minor safety hazards where likely results would be cuts, bruises, etc. Impact of Deadlines: Routine deadlines. Usually sufficient lead time. Variance in work volume, seasonal, and predictable. Priorities can be anticipated. Some interruptions are present. Involves occasional exposure to demands/pressures from persons other than immediate sup. Standing: Daily Walking: Daily Sitting: Daily Reaching with hands and arms: Daily Climbing or balancing: Never Stooping: Occasionally Use of hands to handle objects: Daily Lifting up to 10lbs: Daily Lifting up to 25lbs: Never Lifting over 25lbs: Never Talking as express or exchange of ideas verbally: Daily Hearing as perceive sound by ear: Daily Vision as distinguish colors & depth: Daily Special Requirements or Considerations of the Job: This position requires a criminal background screening. Special Instructions to Applicants: Candidates must apply online through the University of West Florida website: https://careers.uwf.edu. Applicants are required to attach a cv, letter of interest and contact information for three references. References will be contacted upon submission of this application. An opportunity to upload these documents will be provided during the application process. For assistance, please contact Human Resources at 850.474.2694 or email jobs@uwf.edu. If one or more of your advanced degrees is awarded from an institution outside of the United States, UWF will require a credential evaluation and/or translation prior to onboarding. This credential evaluation should include original documents received directly from your institution so that the agency can evaluate and attest that your credential is equivalent to a similar degree earned at a US institution. If your credential is not in English, this evaluation should also include a translation to US English. UWF does not endorse any particular approved agency; however, typically any current National Association of Credential Evaluation Services (NACES) member agency is acceptable. You are advised to check with the agency first to make sure your educational background can be evaluated as some agencies do not evaluate all international degrees. When necessary, these credential evaluations will be requested as part of the Offer Letter with instructions on where and to whom these documents should be submitted. ADDITIONAL POSTING DETAILS Number of Vacancies: 1 Work Hours: 8:00 am - 5:00 pm FTE: 1.0 Preferred Response Date: 01/11/2027 The University of West Florida is an Equal Opportunity/Access/Disabled/Veteran employer. Any individual requiring special accommodation to apply is requested to advise UWF by contacting UWF Human Resources at 850.474.2694. E-Verify is required for employment. All applications for employment at the University are subject to Florida public records law. Apply First Name (required) f500c090 Last Name (required) 45c3ba4e Email (required) d660ac6c Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Apply Now First Name (required) c4a92c75 Last Name (required) 34c04a6f Email (required) 3970020c Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you FOLLOW US Accessibility Equal Opportunity About This Site Contact Copyright © 2026 The University of West Florida All Rights Reserved | Disclaimer and Privacy Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalize content such as job recommendations, and to analyze our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device. For more information, see UWF's Internet Privacy Statement . Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us to know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. If you do not allow these cookie we will not know when you have visited our site, and will not be able to monitor its performance. Manage Cookies I do not accept I accept Save"
