    "https://careers.uwf.edu/jobs/assistant-director-125630-pensacola-main-campus-florida-united-states": {
      "title": "Assistant Director 125630",
      "description": "Assistant Director 125630 - Pensacola - Main Campus, Florida, United States My settings Life at UWF About UWF UWF News Pensacola Area Benefits Wellness Program Search Jobs Job Alerts Faculty Jobs Staff Jobs Adjunct Jobs Temporary Jobs Student Jobs Applicant FAQs My Application Kiosk mode We're Hiring! Please review job requirements carefully. Failure to submit all pieces of the application may result in disqualification. Applications are accepted on the University of West Florida career site . For assistance contact UWF Human Resources at 850.474.2694 or jobs@uwf.edu . Assistant Director 125630 Pensacola - Main Campus, Florida, United States WUWF - Mgmt & General Administrator Staff Full-time POSITION DETAILS Employment based visa sponsorship will NOT be considered for this position. Position Number: 125630 Classification Title: Assistant Director Working Title: Assistant Director, Multimedia Production Department: WUWF - Mgmt & General FLSA Status: Exempt Salary Range: $65,262 - $71,195 Pay Basis: Annually POSITION OVERVIEW JOB SUMMARY: The Assistant Director, Multimedia Production builds and runs the creative production system through which WUWF makes multimedia work, develops student producers and carries out university and community partnerships. This is a producer's job, a coach's job and a partner-facing job rather than a desk-management role: the incumbent conceives a strong piece, shoots or directs it, edits it, builds the crew around it, and teaches students to do more of that work themselves. The role spans video, audio, live and digital production, with RadioLive and WUWF's live events at its center. It produces the station's recurring spot and promo inventory, builds a recurring student production cohort, and serves as WUWF's principal day-to-day production partner for university units and external clients. The position reports to the Director of Operations & Technology and works closely with Content & Audience. MINIMUM QUALIFICATIONS: Masters degree in an appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience: or a bachelors degree in an appropriate area of specialization and four years of appropriate experience. POSITION QUALIFICATIONS: Master's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and two years of appropriate experience; or a bachelor's degree in an appropriate area of specialization and four years of appropriate experience. A portfolio demonstrating strong creative judgment and hands-on execution across multiple forms of production. Fluency with professional camera, lighting, audio and non-linear editing workflows, sufficient to produce high-quality work personally and coach others in the craft. Experience leading productions or crews, including responsibility for schedules, assignments, quality control and final delivery. Demonstrated ability to coach, supervise or develop students, interns, early-career creators or less-experienced crew members. Experience working with clients, partners or internal stakeholders and turning feedback into a finished product without losing production discipline. Strong project-management judgment under competing deadlines, including the ability to say what a production requires and when capacity is insufficient. Ability to work regular evening and weekend productions and occasional travel as the assignment requires. PREFERRED QUALIFICATIONS: Live audio, podcast, studio or live-music production experience. Live multi-camera production, streaming, control-room or venue production experience. Sports-media, athletics-production or game-presentation experience. Motion-graphics, visual-design or advanced post-production capability. Experience building repeatable student, intern or freelance production crews rather than relying solely on individual craft. Experience in higher education, or in a professional environment that regularly integrates students into real production work. Experience producing social and short-form digital content as part of a broader multiplatform strategy. Graduate degree or active graduate study in communication, media, journalism, production or a related field. ESSENTIAL FUNCTIONS/JOB DUTIES: 40% - Production: RadioLive, live events and multimedia. Produce and direct WUWF's live and event productions, with RadioLive as the flagship, to a broadcast-ready standard, with clear run-of-show, crew assignments, communications plans and contingencies. Produce, direct, shoot and edit high-value multimedia work across video, audio, live, studio and digital formats, remaining a hands-on practitioner rather than only assigning work to others. Provide video production for WUWF podcasts, series and other original content. Editorial priorities and approval sit with Content & Audience; production planning, crews, craft and delivery sit here. Translate editorial and audience goals into creative concepts, treatments and production plans, and lead pre-production and post-production on complex projects, including crew assignments, schedules, locations, releases, technical requirements, edit review and final delivery. Set and model professional standards for visual storytelling, sound, pacing, graphics, framing, lighting and production value, matching effort to the importance and lifespan of the work, and develop recurring formats that staff and students can produce efficiently. Produce recorded spots and promos for the station's recurring inventory, including underwriting and day-sponsor credits, membership and pledge spots, promotional announcements, learning-minute segments and event spots. Revenue & Development leads this work and brings in on-air talent to voice; this role provides recording, editing, mixing and delivery. Plan for the realities of live work (evenings, weekends, travel, rapid turnaround and equipment failure) without compromising safety or core station obligations. - (Essential) 25% - Student production and applied learning. Build and supervise a recurring student production cohort that can return and deepen across semesters rather than starting from zero with each internship cycle. Recruit students across Communication Arts and other academic programs, and design onboarding that makes expectations, professional standards, crew roles and pathways to advancement explicit. Coach students from novice through advanced work in camera, audio, lighting, editing, live production, digital storytelling, set etiquette and production problem-solving, giving specific feedback and ensuring every supervised placement produces genuine learning, meaningful responsibility and portfolio-quality artifacts. Create a competency ladder that moves reliable students from shadowing to discrete assignments, including supervised partner projects, to crew leadership and increasingly independent production responsibility. Develop student crews capable of performing defined live-production roles consistently under deadline and public-facing conditions. Maintain the faculty relationships that allow practica, course partnerships and internships to recur; support or teach a production practicum if an academic appointment is established. Track student participation, skills, assignments and portfolio work so WUWF can demonstrate the university value the program creates. - (Essential) 25% - Partnerships and client production. Serve as WUWF's principal day-to-day production partner for Athletics, Marketing & Communications, other university units and external clients, converting partner needs into executable production plans. Produce or direct partner work, including sports and university events, coordinating with Athletics Communications and other partners on game-day and event needs while keeping brand authority with the partner and production execution with the WUWF-led team. Scope projects within approved priorities, budgets and capacity: define audience, purpose, deliverables, deadlines, approvals, production load, student-learning value and the responsibilities of each partner. Estimate staffing, equipment, travel and other resource requirements, and elevate significant new commitments and capacity conflicts early to leadership. Build repeatable production relationships with clear calendars, intake processes, handoffs and expectations, so partner projects are well planned and reliably delivered. Help develop and apply WUWF's framework for university production partnerships, including scope, capacity and cost recovery, and agreements with large recurring partners. - (Essential) 10% - Department management and production systems. Supervise assigned staff, including the Station Technology & Digital Support position (109790), and student, OPS and freelance production personnel: set expectations, assign work, evaluate performance and support development. Manage assigned production budgets, student and OPS hours, freelance support and project resources with clear documentation and cost discipline. Maintain production workflows that are understandable and repeatable across staff and student crews: project intake, file naming, media management, review, delivery and archive. Keep production equipment organized, deployable and documented in coordination with the Director of Operations & Technology, and recommend equipment, software, platform and technical-standard needs through that director. Evaluate new production tools, including AI-assisted tools, where they can improve speed or quality without undermining rights, accuracy, transparency or professional judgment. Represent WUWF in production-related university and community relationships, surface opportunities that advance both public service and student learning, and contribute production, partnership and student-engagement results to station planning and leadership reporting, including what should be scaled, changed or stopped. - (Essential) PHYSICAL DEMANDS: Physical Requirements: Occasional and/or light lifting required. Limited exposure to elements such as heat, cold, noise, dust, dirt, chemicals, etc., but none to the point of being disagreeable. May involve minor safety hazards where likely results would be cuts, bruises, etc. Impact of Deadlines: Externally imposed deadlines set or revised on short notice. Frequent shifts in priority. Numerous interruptions requiring immediate attention. Unusual pressure on a daily basis due to accountability for success of major projects. Standing: Daily Walking: Daily Sitting: Daily Reaching with hands and arms: Daily Climbing or balancing: Occasionally Stooping: Occasionally Use of hands to handle objects: Daily Lifting up to 10lbs: Occasionally Lifting up to 25lbs: Occasionally Lifting over 25lbs: Occasionally Talking as express or exchange of ideas verbally: Daily Hearing as perceive sound by ear: Daily Vision as distinguish colors & depth: Daily Special Requirements or Considerations of the Job: This position requires a criminal background screening. This position requires fingerprinting. Special Instructions to Applicants: Candidates must apply online through the University of West Florida website: https://careers.uwf.edu. Applicants are required to attach a resume, cover letter and contact information for three references. An opportunity to upload these documents will be provided during the application process. For assistance, please contact Human Resources at 850.474.2694 or email jobs@uwf.edu. ADDITIONAL POSTING DETAILS Number of Vacancies: 1 Work Hours: 9:00 am - 6:00 pm FTE: 1.0 Preferred Response Date: 11/13/2026 The University of West Florida is an Equal Opportunity/Access/Disabled/Veteran employer. Any individual requiring special accommodation to apply is requested to advise UWF by contacting UWF Human Resources at 850.474.2694. E-Verify is required for employment. All applications for employment at the University are subject to Florida public records law. Apply First Name (required) 817295a0 Last Name (required) e38bc719 Email (required) 80abad55 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you Apply Now First Name (required) 921ffd56 Last Name (required) 1b5934a9 Email (required) 15b822f5 Apply Please wait... Done Not You? Thank you FOLLOW US Accessibility Equal Opportunity About This Site Contact Copyright © 2026 The University of West Florida All Rights Reserved | Disclaimer and Privacy Candidate Details This website uses cookies. We use cookies to personalize content such as job recommendations, and to analyze our traffic. You consent to our cookies if you click \"I Accept\". If you click on \"I Do Not Accept\", then we will not use cookies but you may have a deteriorated user experience. You can change your settings by clicking on the Settings link on the top right of the device. For more information, see UWF's Internet Privacy Statement . Manage Consent Preferences Strictly Necessary Cookies These cookies are necessary for the website to function and cannot be switched off in our systems. They are usually only set in response to actions made by you which amount to a request for services, such as setting your privacy preferences, logging in or filling in forms. You can set your browser to block or alert you about these cookies, but some parts of the site will not then work. Performance Cookies These cookies allow us to count visits and traffic sources so we can measure and improve the performance of our site. They help us to know which pages are the most and least popular and see how visitors move around the site. If you do not allow these cookie we will not know when you have visited our site, and will not be able to monitor its performance. Manage Cookies I do not accept I accept Save"
